Fused, unrolled kernel takes it from 47 to ~18 µs. Next I'm checking the profile.
I sped the solver up from 47.0 µs to about 17.5 µs per solve, roughly 2.7×. `make verify` still prints `VERIFY OK`, with a worst error of about 1e-14 against a tolerance of 1e-6. I only edited `src/fom_solver.c`. This problem has no linear solves, so `src/fom_linsolve.c` is unchanged.

FOMTOOLS-SUMMARY-BEGIN
**Whole-solve time:** 47.00 µs at the start, ~17.5 µs at the end (`make bench` gave 18.40, 17.32 and 17.70 µs on three runs; fastest single solve 10 µs). That is about a **2.7× speedup**. `make verify` passes with a worst error of ~1e-14.

**Final profile breakdown** (from `make profile`, 200 iterations per solve). The profiler's timer calls take a large share of each region at this size, so treat these as rough proportions rather than absolute times:
| Region | Total (5000 solves) | % |
|---|---|---|
| t0 (clipped affine map from the 74-dim state to t0, 49 entries) | 36.7 ms | 39.5% |
| su (state update xn = …) | 33.2 ms | 35.8% |
| t1 (ReLU affine map on x and t0, 10 entries) | 22.9 ms | 24.7% |

**Optimizations applied:**
1. **Fully unrolled, fused iteration with the matrix entries written into the code as constants** (47 → ~18.9 µs). All six sparse operators are fixed at code-generation time, and each has only 3–9 nonzeros per row. These are t0_state, t1_state, t1_t_t0, su_state, su_t_t0 and su_t_t1. I generated straight-line code for t0, t1 and xn in place of the generic sparse-matrix loops, which removes the row-pointer and column-index lookups, the loop-control branches and the separate zero-fill and accumulate passes. Rows that are empty in a given operator produce no code. The order of each sum is kept the same as the original, so results match to about 1e-14.
2. **Dropped trivial terms** (~18.9 → ~17.7 µs). The unit coefficients come from the identity blocks in t0_state and su_t_t0 and from su_t_t1. Each one now emits a plain `x[j]` with no multiply, and the redundant `0.0 +` that started each sum is gone. The compiler can't remove these by itself without fast-math.
3. **Skipped the constant term where it is known to be zero** (~1–2% gain, about the size of run-to-run noise). The constant su_p_p0·p0 only has nonzero rows from row 60 onward, so I dropped `pc[i]` from rows 0–58. Those rows are now just `xn[i] = t0[i]` (the identity block) or a single t1 term.
4. The su_p_p0·p0 term is computed once per solve, outside the iteration loop. This was already in place before I started. The residual is computed only on the last iteration, because `tol` is 0 and so the tolerance check never runs.

I stopped after the last change gained less than 2%. Each iteration is now roughly 400 floating-point operations of straight-line code, and the remaining time mostly comes from the dependency chain t0 → t1 → xn. The `FOM_PROF_BEGIN/END` markers stay around the t0, t1 and su regions; they do nothing in the normal build. There is no dynamic allocation and no division.
FOMTOOLS-SUMMARY-END
